+++ b/DIRECT_TABLE_MRE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,141 @@
+# Table-aware Direct-Arena ABI
+
+This branch adds a table-aware API beside the existing per-row
+`DirectCallable`. Portable descriptors and checked call-view validation are
+architecture-neutral. Generated scalar and SIMD table callables are currently
+implemented only for AArch64.
+
+## Two-table ABI
+
+`DirectTableApplicationMetadata` authenticates byte offsets in fixed-width
+opaque rows:
+
+- an invocation row supplies every split input-plane descriptor index plus an
+  attachment start/count;
+- an attachment row supplies one destination-plane field for every split
+  source output component, one complex-factor index, and `Initialize` or
+  `Add`.
+
+An attachment therefore applies one factor/operation to the complete complex
+output vector. Multiple attachment rows provide whole-output fanout. Source
+states are implicit leading plane bindings. Every source parameter has an
+authenticated `Plane(binding)` or `Scalar(catalog_index)` mapping, so
+point-varying planes and fixed scalar broadcasts can be mixed.
+
+The logical execution order is exact:
+
+```text
+for invocation row:
+    for point:
+        execute the inline source body
+        for attachment in that row's declared order:
+            for complex output in source order:
+                Initialize/Add destination = complex_factor * output
+```
+
+The host dispatcher keeps invocation rows outermost when it splits an
+unaligned range into scalar head, NEON middle, and scalar tail calls. That
+detail is required for shifted cross-row aliases: an entire earlier row
+finishes before a later row starts.
+
+## Ordered alias contract
+
+Checked calls compare complete descriptor ranges, not just pointer equality or
+the active point window.
+
+- Within one invocation row, every input/destination alias and every
+  destination/destination collision is rejected, including shifted partial
+  overlaps and collisions between different outputs of one attachment.
+- Across different rows, ordered aliases are accepted. A later row may consume
+  an earlier output, overwrite an input whose earlier row has expired, reuse an
+  attachment row, or accumulate/fan out into a destination written by an
+  earlier row.
+- Scalar values, factor arrays, invocation/attachment tables, and
+  plane/scalar descriptor catalogs remain live globally. Any destination that
+  overlaps one of those buffers is rejected.
+
+The row-local validator uses no temporary heap collections. After warmup, both
+checked and unchecked calls remain allocation-free.
+
+Layout and metadata fields are public descriptor data and may be mutated by a
+caller after construction. The public standalone
+`DirectTableApplicationMetadata::validate_call_view` entry point therefore
+reruns complete metadata/layout validation before it inspects any row pointer.
+Constructed applets privately own already-authenticated metadata, so their
+checked hot path can retain zero-allocation validation safely.
+
+## Portable descriptor codec
+
+`DirectTableApplicationMetadata::encode_descriptor` and
+`decode_descriptor(source, bytes)` implement
+`symjit-direct-table-descriptor-v1`. `DirectTableApplication::from_descriptor`
+is the source-first construction path.
+
+The independently loaded immutable source is authoritative for state,
+parameter, and split-output counts. The descriptor contains only an eight-byte
+magic followed by little-endian `u32` fields: version, declared byte length,
+source counts, row strides, field counts/offsets, scalar count, and two-`u32`
+parameter binding records. It contains no native `usize`, executable bytes, or
+raw pointers.
+
+Input, output-component, and parameter counts are each capped at 256, row
+strides at 16,384 bytes, and the complete descriptor at 16 KiB. Decoding checks
+the declared length and loaded-source counts before reserving bounded vectors,
+then rejects unknown versions/kinds, truncation, trailing bytes, out-of-range
+or duplicate fields, malformed complex output pairs, and arithmetic overflow.
+Descriptor encode/decode and source validation do not require AArch64.
+
+## Generated AArch64 control flow
+
+The AArch64 scalar and two-lane NEON functions resolve the current row's input
+plane pointers, execute the remapped MIR body inline once per point block, and
+walk the row's attachments. Every complex source output is stored in its own
+fixed spill pair and each attachment emits all output pairs in source order.
+
+The compiler calls `Mir::rerun` exactly once per scalar/SIMD machine function.
+The call view contains no legacy `CompiledFunc`; focused code-shape tests find
+no AArch64 `BL`/`BLR` and zero packet, gather, or scatter materializations.
+Generated scalar and SIMD frames are checked against `Config::stack_limit()`
+before executable memory is created.
+
+## Current boundary
+
+- AArch64 scalar and NEON `f64x2` callable generation and execution are
+  implemented.
+- Descriptor encoding/decoding, source validation, row layouts, and checked
+  alias validation are portable.
+- x86-64 has **no callable implementation yet**: there is no scalar, SSE, or
+  AVX table generator. A non-AArch64 host may decode and validate descriptors,
+  but `DirectTableApplication::seal` fails explicitly.
+- Source MIR must remain call-free and branch-free; external-call and
+  branch-divergence fallbacks are not implemented.
+- The descriptor codec does not yet provide a canonical digest, prepared-pack
+  emission, or recurrence-runtime pack loading.
+
+Focused tests cover one and multiple complex outputs, whole-output fanout,
+non-unit factors, both destination operations, exact row/attachment/output
+order, cross-row producer/consumer and expired-input aliases, attachment reuse,
+shifted descriptor bases, odd scalar tails, same-row and global-buffer
+rejections, malformed/oversized descriptor bytes, fixed scalar broadcasts,
+mixed plane/scalar parameters, large spill frames, signed zero, finite
+extremes, and exact quiet-NaN payload bits.
+
+## Prepared-eager evidence
+
+Tests embed the pinned 360-byte built-in-SM recurrence-canary payload
+`kernels/000006/application-0.symjit`, SHA-256
+`558b681aab77670552237c3ca544cc618a23851586c244e873188921b48d73ab`.
+The payload has six complex inputs and two complex outputs. The untouched O3
+payload now executes through one table attachment containing all four split
+destination fields and fans the complete output vector to two destination
+sets; every active component is compared bit-for-bit with ordinary O3 dense
+evaluation. A separate retained-first-output path continues to exercise the
+O2 exact-factor per-row oracle.
+
+The full two-output prepared callable is also the warmed allocation
+regression: 128 checked and 128 unchecked calls allocate zero while retaining
+the odd seven-point NEON tail and zero materialization code shape.
+
+This standalone branch establishes the hardened ABI behavior. It does not
+claim that prepared-pack wiring, x86 callable generation, or authoritative
+recurrence-runtime integration is complete.
